  "source_dataset": "MIMIC_IV_NOTE",
  "text": "Allergies:\nNo Known Allergies / Adverse Drug Reactions\n\nChief Complaint:\nabcess, wound care, and epistaxis\n \n___ Surgical or Invasive Procedure:\n- Removal of abdominal wound retention sutures\n- Incision and drainage of left chest wall abscess\n- Removal of nasal Rhino rocket packing\n\nHistory of Present Illness:\nMr. ___ is a ___ with a PMHx of schizophrenia/bipolar \ndisorder, asthma, and HTN, s/p recent exlap appendectomy who is \nbeing transferred for wound care and management of epistaxis.  \n\nHe was recently admitted to ___ (___) with \nperforated gangrenous appendicitis and purulent peritonitis, s/p \nexlap and appendectomy (c/b fascial dehiscence on POD5, s/p \nwound exploration fascial closure and placement of retention \nsutures on ___. His course was also c/b L subclavian line \ninfection, fevers and tachycardia. He was transferred to ___ \non ___ for management of sepsis and ___. He received 1U PRBCs, \nbroad antibiosis and ultimately discharged on Cipro on ___.  \n\n\nHe was readmitted to ___ on ___ with lethargy, \nweakness, and pus draining from left chest wall. His labs on \nthis admission notable for Hgb 8.4, WBC 8 (normal diff), Plt \n125, Cr 2.2 (1.9 on ___. Chest CT showed left infraclvicular \nsoft tissue abscess inflammation. Abdominal CT showed ascites, \nsoft tissue stranding, and a possible 2cm collection. He \nunderwent I&D and packing of L chest wall. Sutures were removed \nfrom the umbilicus (retention sutures remain in place) and his \nabdominal wound is draining seromatous fluid, which may need a \nVac dressing. He has been hemodynamically stable on empiric vanc \nand meropenem (started upon discussion with ___ ID), while \nblood and tissue cultures are NGTD. His course has been \ncomplicated by bilateral nose bleeds and he has Rhino packing \nbilaterally, which will remain in for 5 days (per discussion \nwith ___ ENT). Hb 7.7 today following epistaxis. Cr 1.86 \nfollowing IVF. The patient requires ENT, ID, and general surgery \nconsultation for wound care (ENT and ID not available at ___), \nfor which he is transferred to ___.  \nOn arrival to the ___ medical floor, the patient is \ncomfortable without acute complaints. Initial VS 97.8, 131/97, \n98, 18, 98%RA. He has some pain at the L clavicular I&D site, \nminimal abdominal pain, mostly from constipation. Otherwise, \ndenies any chest pain, dyspnea, N/V, fevers/chills.\n\nBrief Hospital Course:\n___ M with a PMHx of schizophrenia/bipolar disorder, asthma, \nand HTN, recent admission for gangrenous appendicitis and \npurulent peritonitis s/p ex-lap and appendectomy with \ncomplicated post-op course, readmitted to ___ with \nsurgical wound infections and being transferred to ___ for \nwound care and management of unremitting epistaxis.  \n\nActive Issues this Admission:\n\n# Subclavian Line Site Infection - line placed during admission \nfor appendectomy c/b infection and eventually developed a fluid \ncollection/abscess. The abscess was drained and packed. The \npatient was initially placed on Meropenem and Vancomycin for a \nculture from the OSH reportedly growing coag negative staph. \nThis was discontinued on ___ as the patient was afebrile and the \nerythema surrounding the wound had resolved. A second set of \ncultures from ___ also grew Coag negative staph sensitive to \nClindamycin and the patient was placed on a 5 day course of \nclindamycin (last dose ___ and continued wound \npacking/management. The wound site was healing well at time of \ndischarge.\n\n# Surgical Wound Dehiscence - The patient had an \nex-lap/appendectomy on ___ at ___ which was \ncomplicated by facial dehiscence and placement of retention \nsutures. Acute Care Surgery was consulted at ___ and they \nremoved the retention sutures and confirmed that the facial \nplane was intact. There initial ___ treatment was also to \ncover the abdominal would but surgery and the primary team did \nnot feel that the site was actively infected and this was \ndiscontinued. The patient is to follow up with Acute Care \nsurgery for his wound management.\n\n# Epistaxis - patient presented following bilateral epistaxis \nand placement of rhino rockets at the OSH. ENT was consulted \nthis admission and it was felt that the epistaxis was due to an \nelevated INR. The nasal packing was left in for 5 days and the \npatient was placed on prophylaxis for toxic shock syndrome. ENT \nremoved the nasal packing on day five and will have the patient \nfollow up in clinic. Will also d/c patient on bowel regimen as \nENT reccomends that that the patient not strain with bowel \nmovements.\n\n# Elevated INR - On presentation, INR was elevated. This was \nfelt to be most likely due to malnutrition in the setting of \nantibiotic use, given LFTs WNL. INR has normalized on PO Vit K.\n\n# Acute on chronic renal failure- the patient presented with an \nelevated creatinine. His baseline is unknown. This trended down \nduring the admission and was 1.5. \n\n# Anemia - At discharge, hgb stable at 8.6 which is not \nsignificantly changed from prior discharge levels. His anemia \nwas thought to be due to a combination of iron deficiency ___ \nblood loss from surgery as well as inflammation.\n\nChronic Issues:\n\nSchizophrenia/Bipolar DO - the patient was maintained on his \nhome clozaril for the duration of the hospitalization with no \ndecompensation or issues.\n\nTransitional Issues:\n\n- complete clindamycin ___\n- continue BID abdominal wound and subclavicular wound dressing \nchanges\n- f/u with Acute Care surgery\n- f/u with ENT\n- f/u with PCP to evaluate for elevated creatinine\n\nDischarge Diagnosis:\nPrimary:\n- Coagulase negative staph left chest wall abscess (POA)\n- Post-operative abdominal wound dehiscense (POA)\n- Epistaxis\n- Acute on chronic renal failure (resolved, baseline now 1.5)\n- Anemia (inflammation +/- iron deficiency)\n- Constipation\n- Coagulopathy (nutritional)\n\nSecondary:\n- Schizophrenia\n- Hypertension\n- Asthma  \n- Thrombocytopenia NOS\n- Perforated gangrenous appendicitis s/p exploratory laparotomy, \nappendectomy, washout, placement of drains ___ ___ \n___\n- Post-op wound dehiscense s/p wound exploration, removal of JP \ndrains, fascial closure, placement of retention sutures ___ \nSSH)\n- Left subclavian CVL-associated SSTI/Abscess (___) \n\nConsultations:\n- General Surgery: Dr. ___, MD\n- Otolaryngology: Dr. ___, MD",
